Gemstones have a rich body of lore surrounding them, as do most things
that have been around us for hundreds of years and look suitably
mystical and sparkly.  The 1st Edition AD&D handbook has a long list
of gemstones and uses for them.

My suggestion is to take advantage of this.  Why should luckstones get
all the *-fun?  The idea is to turn most of the real (not colored
glass) gem types into individual (something)-stones, each for a
different thing.

Actually I probably overstated my idea there, I'm not talking about
giving gemstones *obvious* effects, certainly nothing that would show
up on the Probing/Enlightenment/End Game intrinsic lists.  I'm talking
about little, slight things.

For example.  Agate is supposedly good for insects bites.  So,
carrying an agate stone would provide protection against the poison
from soldier ants and killer bees.  Either something small (a +2 to
poison saves?) or something big but infrequent (protection from the
deadly-poison of those stings).

Like luckstones, the effect would come just from being carried, and
would provide a subtle, "background" effect that isn't mentioned in
ordinary play, and also like luckstones, putting them in any sort of
container removes the effect (at least, I think luckstones do
this....).  Unlike luckstones, even the most powerful of gemstones
only provides a modest benefit: as a yardstick for determining power,
anything beyond an effective universal +1 to luck would probably be
too much, though somewhat-larger bonuses that only affect one kind of
check are okay.  Finally, having more than one of a gemstone out
carries no additional benefit.

The first problem with this: Well, the legends surrounding gemstones
are thick and overlapping.  A large number of them seem to have some
connection with stomach disorders (according to:
http://www.findyourfate.com/gemology/superstition.htm).  I would say
to arbitrarily pick one "use" for each gem that isn't provided by any
of the others, which is less, um, "realistic," but would provide for
better gameplay.

The second problem with this: gemstones are rare, but light, and once
found it's easy to keep a bunch out and soak up the benefits.  One
counter for this: naturally, cursed gemstones would have bad or
opposite effects.  Another, better, counter: having a lot of gemstones
out makes it easy to fill up your knapsack's letter-space, eventually
forcing the player to choose between easy, continuous effects, or
keeping more equipment out for quick use.

Some suggestions, adapted from the above page, with a couple of others
thrown in:

* Agate: Good against poison from insects.
* Amber: Slight protection against sickness.
* Amethyst: Already has enough in-game use.
* Dilithium Crystal: Energy source.  (Slightly increases the rate of
power recovery.)
* Aquamarine: "Brings wisdom, success and popularity." 
* Diamond: "A girl's best friend."  Helps slightly in encounters with
succubi.  (I don't know which gem would have a counter effect with
incubi, so this might break gender parity.)
* Emerald: Protection against memory loss -- fewer things forgotten
when a scroll of amnesia is read or your brain gets sucked.
* Garnet: Repels flying insects?  Protection against lightning?
* Jacinth/Zircon: Strengthens the heart - exercises constitution.
* Jade: Good against eye troubles.  Remedy for digestive ailments. 
Cures animal bites.
* Jasper: Treats epilepsy (increases saving throw against sleep
attacks)
* Opal: *Unlucky* except in October, or when a diamond is carried. 
(According to the page, also very unlucky with an engagement ring.)
* Ruby: Prot. against evil.  "Peace of mind, courage, insight, mental
power."  Stomach-ache.  Antidote to snake poison & harms poisonous
reptiles.  Healing.
* Sapphire: Strong against spiders.  (Poison?  Webs?)
* Topaz: Loyal and faithful friendships - reduced rate of tameness
decrease for pets.
* Turquoise: Protects against falls when riding.
* Aquamarine: Exercises wisdom.
* Black Opal: Lucky.

Remaining, unsuggested gemstones: citrine, jet, chrysoberyl, fluorite,
obsidian.

